{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central mechanism relies on the claim that surviving severe tests demonstrates a 'higher degree of verisimilitude,' making the preference defensible. This contradicts Popper's own later admission that his definition of verisimilitude failed and that we can never know if a theory is closer to the truth than another based solely on corroboration. By grounding the defense in verisimilitude, the target smuggles in the very inductive assumption Popper sought to avoid: that past survival indicates a greater likelihood of truth-content (verisimilitude), which is an inductive inference about the relationship between test performance and truth approximation. If verisimilitude cannot be assessed without induction, then using it to justify action collapses the distinction Popper tried to maintain, failing the problem's requirement to defend the preference strictly on 'Popper's own terms' without smuggling induction back in.","premise":"Popper explicitly rejected verisimilitude (truth-likeness) as a viable basis for preferring theories because it cannot be non-inductively established that a better-corroborated theory has higher verisimilitude.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be non-inductively measured, what non-inductive metric does Popper actually offer to justify acting on one theory over another without implying it is 'better' in a truth-tracking sense?","target":"dc653385c425a2b4859f36752a1d12199a138de52030b69b264170ab12c73b3b"}]}